course: everyday
lesson: 5
title: Riding a bike or a scooter safely
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A helmet protects your brain if you fall; it only works if it fits: level on your head, low on your forehead (about two fingers above your eyebrows), straps forming a V under each ear and the chin strap snug, so it does not slide when you shake your head.
explain: On the road, ride in the same direction as the traffic: on the right in the USA and Canada, on the left in the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand.
explain: Tell others what you will do with hand signals: before you turn, point your arm straight out to the side you are turning to, and look behind you first.
explain: Make sure you are seen: bright clothes by day, and at night a white light at the front, a red light at the back and reflective things on you and the bike.
explain: Drivers often do not see children, so try to catch the driver's eye; a boy in Austin waits at a driveway until the reversing car has stopped and the driver has seen him.
explain: A boy in Florida rode his bike without a helmet down a hill; he hit a crack in the path, flew over the handlebars and hurt his head so badly he needed hospital for many days.
explain: A girl in London rode past a row of parked cars; a driver opened a door right in front of her, she hit it and fell into the road, but her helmet and a careful driver behind saved her from worse.
fact: A bike helmet protects your head and brain when you fall.
fact: A helmet should sit level and low on your forehead, about two fingers above your eyebrows.
fact: The chin strap should be snug, with only a finger or two fitting under it.
fact: A helmet that has had a hard crash should be replaced, even if it looks fine.
fact: Cyclists ride in the same direction as the traffic.
fact: Traffic drives on the right in the USA and Canada and on the left in the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand.
fact: To signal a turn, a cyclist holds an arm straight out to the side they will turn to.
fact: Bikes at night need a white light at the front and a red light at the back.
fact: Bright clothes help drivers see you by day, and reflective clothes shine in headlights at night.
fact: Headphones stop you hearing cars and bells, so you should not wear them while riding.
fact: Scooter riders need a helmet too.
fact: Brakes should be checked before a ride.
fact: A helmet that fits well can more than halve the chance of a serious head injury in a bike crash.
fact: Many cycling crashes happen when a car turns across a rider or a parked car's door opens suddenly.
exercise: Why do you wear a helmet on a bike or scooter? | To protect your head if you fall | To keep your hair tidy and out of your eyes | To make you go faster | To keep the rain off
exercise: Where should a well fitted helmet sit? | Level and low on the forehead | Tipped back to show the whole forehead | Hanging behind the head | Sideways over one ear
exercise: How tight should the chin strap be? | Snug, with a finger or two under it | So loose that it hangs down | So tight you cannot open your mouth | Not done up at all
exercise: Your helmet hit the road hard in a crash but looks fine. What should you do? | Get a new helmet | Keep using it, it looks fine | Paint over the scratches | Tighten the straps more
exercise: Which way should you ride on a road? | In the same direction as the traffic | Against the traffic, to see the cars | In the middle of the road | Whichever way is shorter
exercise: In Canada, on which side of the road do you ride? | The right | The left | The middle | Either side
exercise: In Australia, on which side of the road do you ride? | The left | The right | The middle | Either side
exercise: How do you show you are about to turn left? | Hold your left arm straight out | Ring your bell twice | Hold your right arm straight up | Nod your head to the left
exercise: What should you do just before you signal and turn? | Look behind you | Close your eyes for a moment | Speed up as fast as you can | Take both hands off the handlebars
exercise: What lights does a bike need at night? | White at the front and red at the back | Red at the front and white at the back | Green at the front and blue at the back | Only a light on the helmet
exercise: Why are reflective strips useful at night? | They shine back in car headlights | They make the bike lighter | They keep you warm | They light up the dark road ahead for you to see
exercise: Why should you not wear headphones while cycling? | You cannot hear cars or bells | They make the helmet fit badly | They use up the bike's energy | They make you pedal slower
exercise: Does a child riding a scooter need a helmet? | Yes, a scooter fall can hurt your head too | No, scooters are too slow to fall off and get hurt | Only on scooters with a motor | Only in the rain
exercise: What should you check before a ride? | That the brakes work | That the bell is shiny | That the seat is warm | That the bike is clean
exercise: Why should you try to catch a driver's eye at a crossing? | To be sure the driver has seen you | To be polite and say hello to the driver | So the driver speeds up | It does not matter at all
test: A girl in Dublin is going to turn right at a quiet corner. What does she do? | Look behind, hold her right arm out, then turn | Turn quickly without looking | Hold her left arm out to trick cars | Ride over to the right side of the road first and then turn
test: Jack's helmet is tilted back so his whole forehead shows. What could happen if he falls forward? | His forehead would not be protected | He would be perfectly safe | The helmet would fall forward over his eyes | He would bounce off the ground
test: A boy in Seattle is cycling home at dusk in dark clothes with no lights. What is the biggest danger? | Drivers may not see him | His bike may get cold | He may get lost easily in the dark | His tyres may go flat
test: A family from Texas is cycling on holiday in England. What must they remember? | Ride on the left with the traffic | Ride on the right like at home | Ride against the traffic | Ride in the middle of the lane
test: Mia's chin strap hangs open while she rides. What is the problem? | The helmet could fly off in a fall | It makes her ride too slowly | Open straps are safer | Nothing, the strap is only for looks
test: A car is backing out of a driveway as you ride along the street. What is wisest? | Stop and wait until the driver has seen you | Ride fast behind the car | Ring your bell loudly and keep going past | Ride round the front of it
test: Why is riding against the traffic dangerous? | Cars come at you fast and do not expect you | It is slower for cyclists | It makes your legs tired | It is only dangerous at night
test: Your friend rides with music in both ears. What would you tell him? | He cannot hear a car coming behind him | Music makes him pedal better | Only one ear is a problem; with the other one free he can hear | Music makes bikes faster
test: What would be the best thing to wear for a ride on a grey, rainy afternoon? | A bright jacket with reflective strips | A dark grey raincoat | A black hoodie | Whatever colour, it makes no difference
test: helmet's pushed back on my head, that ok? | No, it should sit low, just above your brows | Yes, back is the comfiest way | Yes, as long as it's on | Yes, if the strap is loose
test: biking home in the dark, lights? | White at the front, red at the back | Red at the front, white at the back | No lights if you wear a hat | Only a light on your helmet
test: wanna listen to music on my ride, ok? | No, you need to hear cars and bells | Yes, it helps you ride straight | Yes, if it is very loud | Only on busy roads
test: crashed my bike, helmet looks fine tho, keep it? | No, replace it after a hard crash | Yes, if it has no scratches | Yes, helmets never wear out or need changing | Yes, just tighten the strap
test: helmet makes my hair look bad, just this once? | No, a crash can happen any day, wear it | Yes, if you ride slowly | Yes, if it is a short ride | Yes, if you ride on the pavement and not the road
test: mate crashed his bike, helmet's cracked but he says he's fine | Tell an adult and he needs a new helmet and a check | He is fine if he can stand up | The helmet still works, keep using it | Tape the crack and ride on
test: riding past parked cars, how close? | Leave space, a door could open in front of you | Ride right next to them to stay out of traffic | It does not matter how close | Ride faster so the doors cannot catch you
test: heard a lad in our town got doored on his bike by a parked car and flew like ten metres!! | The real lesson is to ride clear of parked car doors; «ten metres» is likely exaggerated | The real lesson is that bikes should ride on the pavement always | It proves parked cars are only dangerous at night | Every number in it must be correct because it was so exact
test: my mate says helmets don't do anything, his brother fell with one and still got a bump | A bump does not prove helmets fail; a good helmet greatly lowers the chance of serious head injury | He is right, helmets make no difference at all | He is right, helmets only help on scooters | He is right, so helmets are only for little kids
test: Liam fell off his bike outside school and his trousers ripped, i've got a video, send it round? | No, sharing it would embarrass him; ask if he is okay instead | Yes, because a ripped trouser video is harmless fun | Yes, so everyone knows to laugh at him | Yes, but only after adding funny music
exam: Identify the correct position of a bicycle helmet upon the head of its wearer. | Level and low upon the forehead, approximately two fingers above the eyebrows | Tilted backward so that the whole forehead is exposed | Pushed forward until it covers the eyes | Resting loosely upon the crown, the strap unfastened
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the direction in which a cyclist ought to proceed on the road? | In the same direction as the motor traffic | In the direction opposite to the motor traffic | In whichever direction is the less crowded | Down the centre line between the two lanes
exam: It being given that a bicycle is to be ridden after dark, select the equipment that is required. | A white light at the front and a red light at the rear | A red light at the front and a white light at the rear | Two red lights fixed beneath the saddle | A single white light fixed to the rear wheel
exam: Whereas a helmet has sustained a hard impact in a crash, determine the appropriate course of action. | It should be replaced, even though no damage be visible | It may be retained provided no crack be visible | It should be repainted and thereafter worn as before | It should be worn more loosely in future
